Day 2: Conditional Probability & Bayes’ Theorem

1. Conditional probability
Definition: Conditional probability is the probability of an event occurring when another event has already occurred.
Formula:
\[
P(A\mid B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}
\]

Here:
- \(P(A\mid B)\): Probability of A given B.
- \(P(A\cap B)\): Probability that both A and B occur.
- \(P(B)\): Probability of B, which must be greater than zero.
Example: A fair die is rolled. Given that the result is even, what is the probability that it is 4?
Even outcomes: \(\{2,4,6\}\)
Only one outcome is 4, out of three possible even outcomes.
\[
P(4\mid \text{even})=\frac13
\]

2. Independent events
Two events are independent when the occurrence of one does not change the probability of the other.
Formula:
\[
P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B)
\]

Example: Toss a fair coin twice. What is the probability of getting heads on the first toss and tails on the second?
\[
P(H\cap T)=\frac12\times\frac12=\frac14
\]

Remember: Independent events are not the same as mutually exclusive events. Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together.
